# Copy to .env for local development.

# --- Server-side, and genuinely secret -------------------------------------
# These are read by the Netlify functions at request time. They have no VITE_
# prefix on purpose: Vite inlines any VITE_* variable into the browser bundle,
# which would publish them. `netlify dev` picks them up from this file; plain
# `vite dev` serves no /api/* routes at all, so use mock mode there instead.

# The fal.ai key this deployment generates with. Images, video and the
# "Improve with AI" buttons all run on it, and visitors supply no key of their
# own — which means every generation is billed to whoever sets this.
# FAL_KEY=

# REQUIRED for sign-in. Auth0 says who someone is; Supabase will not take its
# word for it, because row-level security only trusts tokens signed with the
# project's own key. `/api/session` signs one — see netlify/lib/supabaseToken.ts
# — and that same token is what the fal proxy verifies, so without this secret
# nobody can sign in and nobody can generate.
#
# Supabase → Project settings → API → JWT keys → the legacy JWT secret. It has
# no VITE_ fallback and must never gain one: inlining it into the bundle would
# let any visitor mint a session as anybody.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# Which Supabase project this site belongs to. Falls back to VITE_SUPABASE_URL
# below — the same public string — so this is only worth setting to point the
# server at a different project.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxx.supabase.co

# REQUIRED for Google Drive, which is granted at sign-in rather than after it.
# Auth0 holds the user's Google tokens; these are the credentials the functions
# use to exchange a caller's Auth0 token for a Google one through Token Vault.
#
# A machine client of its own, not the SPA's — this call carries a secret, and
# the browser must never be able to make it. Auth0 → Applications → the backend
# API's client. AUTH0_DOMAIN falls back to VITE_AUTH0_DOMAIN below because it is
# the same tenant either way; the secret has no such fallback, by design.
# AUTH0_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID=
# AUTH0_BACKEND_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Which tenant and which API to verify callers against. Both fall back to their
# VITE_ forms — the same public strings — so these are only worth setting to
# point the functions somewhere else. Tokens are verified locally against the
# tenant's published signing keys, so this costs no round trip per request.
# AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-tenant.us.auth0.com
# AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://editor-cat/api

# Skips the session check on /api/fal/* entirely, for running `netlify dev`
# against a checkout with no Supabase project. Never set this on a deployed
# site: it leaves the endpoint open for anyone who finds the URL to generate
# video on your account.
# FAL_PROXY_ALLOW_ANONYMOUS=1

# --- Build-time, and not secret --------------------------------------------
# These ship in the bundle by design. All optional.

# Fakes every provider call locally, so no keys and no network are needed.
# VITE_MOCK_PROVIDERS=1

# REQUIRED for sign-in, and therefore for Drive, which rides on the same login.
# The Auth0 tenant, the SPA application's client id, and the API identifier its
# access tokens are minted for. None is a secret: all three are visible in every
# authorisation request the browser makes.
#
# In Auth0, the application's Allowed Callback URLs, Allowed Logout URLs and
# Allowed Web Origins have to cover wherever this is served from. Unlike Google,
# Auth0 takes a wildcard subdomain — https://*.staging.example.com — which is
# what makes deploy previews work without an entry each. Only ever for a domain
# you own; never a shared one like *.netlify.app, where anyone can deploy a site
# that matches and collect your users' tokens.
# VITE_AUTH0_DOMAIN=your-tenant.us.auth0.com
# VITE_AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
# VITE_AUTH0_AUDIENCE=https://editor-cat/api

# Google API key and Cloud project number, for the Google Picker — which is how
# the user chooses their folder and imports existing media. Without the key
# there is no way to pick a folder, so the editor cannot open. Neither is a
# secret: restrict the key by HTTP referrer in the Cloud console. Enable the
# "Google Picker API" there alongside the Drive API.
#
# This is the one Google allowlist this app still keeps. The Picker calls Google
# straight from the page, so Auth0 is nowhere in that path and every origin the
# app is served from needs covering — https://*.staging.example.com/* included.
# VITE_GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# VITE_GOOGLE_PROJECT_NUMBER=

# Supabase project, where timelines are saved. Set both to enable cloud
# projects; leave both unset to run purely against this browser's storage.
# The anon key is public by design — row-level security is what protects data.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...
